WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:06.150
Most of us don't spend much
time thinking about the grid,

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:09.390
the dizzyingly huge system
of cables, transformers,

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:12.900
and outlets that reliably
powers our lives.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.540
It's an easily ignored,
change-resistant part

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:18.063
of the modern world and
that's the way we like it.

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:22.350
But, as we move towards
less planet-melting ways

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.000
or producing electricity,

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.060
the grid is going to have to evolve too.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:30.630
We're seeing so many countries
and governments announcing

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:33.450
high targets when it
comes to decarbonization

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:35.730
and being climate-neutral.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:38.190
But this is just one side of the coin.

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:41.370
It's not enough to simply
generate green energy.

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:44.190
We also need to make sure
that it's transmitted

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:46.920
and distributed to the end consumers.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:50.790
The grid of today just isn't
equipped for renewables,

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:52.740
so a new kind of grid

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:56.730
is starting to take shape, the supergrid.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:59.070
The idea behind a supergrid is basically,

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:01.410
a grid that connects nations,

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:04.230
not just different parts of a country.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:08.130
And the goal is to try and
maximize the use of renewables

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:10.800
wherever they are generating electricity.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:14.070
It's an ambitious idea and
one that could be necessary

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:17.880
to avoid the worst case
scenarios of climate change.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:21.120
But actually building it
is a tough proposition.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:24.840
I have to say, politics is
the most difficult thing.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:28.080
We are still trying to
struggle each other,

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:30.150
competing each other.

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:33.270
A supergrid seems like a super good idea.

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:35.763
The question is, will it
ever become a reality?

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:44.820
Since the earliest days
of harnessing electricity,

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:46.770
getting power where it needs to go

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:49.020
has been at least as big a challenge

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:50.763
as making it in the first place.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.020
And we're talking about the earliest days,

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.270
since Thomas Edison's first electric grid

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:00.093
in Downtown Manhattan in the 1880s.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:02.700
What Edison gave us,

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:05.970
that system ran on what's
called direct current.

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:10.320
And direct current, you, at
the voltages he was producing,

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:12.390
only goes about a mile.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:14.940
Gretchen Bakke is a
cultural anthropologist

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:18.900
who writes about, among
other things, the grid.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.270
He illuminated office
buildings on Wall Street,

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:26.270
for example, mansions, and
then small factory spaces.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:28.020
But every time you wanted
to go more than a mile,

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:30.600
you needed to build a new power plant.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:31.830
And he thought this was fine.

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:32.880
He was like, "This will be fine.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.100
We'll just build another
one one mile over."

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.170
And then another one and another one.

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:40.080
And it was pretty clear
that probably direct current

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.120
was not going to be the long-term answer.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:44.820
And so, that set up the next problem,

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:48.090
which was, how can we
transmit electricity further?

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:50.430
And the answer to that was given to us

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:52.080
by the famous Nikola Tesla,

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:53.913
who was quite the guy.

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:57.660
Tesla came up with the idea
of an alternating current,

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:00.090
which doesn't just flow in one direction,

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:03.990
it keeps alternating back
and forth on a cable.

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:08.130
Alternating current, it allows
you to increase the voltage

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:09.810
to a very high level,

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:12.420
whereas you couldn't do
it with direct current.

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:14.460
Bump up your current to higher voltage

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:17.850
and you can move your
electricity a lot further.

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:20.280
There was some early rivalry over this.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.800
Edison electrocuted an
elephant to demonstrate

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:25.890
that AC was dangerous
and shouldn't be used.

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:28.563
But ultimately, AC was the clear winner.

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:31.470
Electricity was finally able to break out

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:34.290
of the one-mile barrier.

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:36.330
And that's why alternating current

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:39.420
became the dominant form of transporting

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:41.583
and using electricity world over.

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:45.210
And that's the story of electricity.

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:48.573
Just kidding, actually,
renewables ruined everything.

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:52.980
Solar panels or even a wind farm

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:57.180
are not a corollary to a
coal-burning power plant.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:58.620
They're usually in different places.

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:02.400
So coal, we can bring it
where we want to burn it.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.320
Wind, we have to build where it's windy.

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:05.760
And wind is often happening

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.980
where there's not that many people.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.990
Currently we use alternating current

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:12.000
to be able to move electricity,

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:13.890
but the problem with alternating current

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:16.830
is that if you move it
over a very long distance,

00:04:16.830 --> 00:04:18.960
hundreds or even thousands of miles,

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:22.230
you lose too much of that electricity.

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:24.600
And so, moving it long distances makes it

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:26.460
an uneconomical choice.

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:29.310
If we can't get renewable
energy where it needs to go,

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:32.460
we can't transition
away from fossil fuels.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:34.811
So, we need a better
way to move electrons.

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:43.980
When we're talking about
the history of the grid,

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:47.010
AC technology become the dominant approach

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:50.040
to distribute and to transmit energy.

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:53.730
Nevertheless, the DC
technology did not die.

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:55.623
The opposite is actually the case.

00:04:56.850 --> 00:05:00.630
Jochen Costman is in charge
of onshore DC power systems

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:02.370
at Siemens Energy.

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:04.980
Yes, that's right, DC power,

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:07.830
the loser of the 19th century current wars

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:09.570
is making a comeback.

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:11.640
The solution that has become a consensus

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:14.370
in the industry now is
to use direct current

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:18.630
or more specifically,
high-voltage direct current.

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:23.010
HVDC turns out to be
the best of all worlds,

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:25.980
traveling long distances
without losing as much power

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:27.753
as AC along the way.

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:31.740
DC wasn't able to go
long distances previously

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:34.380
because you couldn't create
a high-voltage version,

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:37.110
but inventions in the past
few decades have enabled us

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:40.380
to have the equipment and
the power electronics needed

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:43.470
to generate high-voltage direct current.

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:46.530
The engineers here make
building-sized machines

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:50.760
that generate that sweet,
sweet high-voltage DC.

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:53.460
They partner with companies
like Italy-based Prysmian,

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:55.560
which make the cables themselves,

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:58.860
long strands of insulated
copper or aluminum

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:01.590
that can span hundreds of miles.

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:05.280
Another important use case
of the HVDC technology

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:08.760
is the interconnections of countries

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:11.490
to allow efficient energy trading

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:13.683
between the countries and the network.

00:06:14.670 --> 00:06:18.840
The supergrid in a way is
already a reality in Europe.

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:21.540
Denmark is connected to Norway.

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:23.490
France is connected to Spain.

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:25.653
Sweden is connected to Germany.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:28.980
Interconnectors let
countries take advantage

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:32.130
of renewable energy wherever
it's being generated.

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:35.160
If one country is having
a particularly windy day,

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:37.910
they can share some of that
power with their neighbors.

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:41.430
That's the idea behind the
most recent interconnector

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:44.400
to be installed, a project called ElecLink

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:46.450
that's rooted through the Channel Tunnel.

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:52.830
An interconnector allows surplus power

00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:55.470
that isn't used in the UK

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:58.770
to be transmitted over to France.

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:01.800
Or alternatively, take
some energy from France

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:04.053
and transmit it back to the UK.

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:06.540
Phillip O'Gorman and his colleagues

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:09.300
have been working on ElecLink
for more than a decade

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:12.150
and finally transmitted
power for the first time

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:13.980
in May this year.

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:18.030
We transmit up to 1,000
megawatts in either direction,

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:21.660
enough to power a million to
a million and a half homes.

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:24.330
You're moving clean energy from a place

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:27.030
where it's in surplus to a
place where it's in demand.

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:28.920
Certainly throughout the world,

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:31.299
we're going to be seeing
a lot more interconnectors

00:07:31.299 --> 00:07:33.960
across the world, across the globe.

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:36.300
There is potential for them to do exactly

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:37.383
as we are doing now.

00:07:39.570 --> 00:07:41.400
But in other parts of the world,

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:43.533
this idea has run into some trouble.

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:51.480
When I was first beginning
to research the grid book,

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:53.580
which was in 2007,

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:56.910
there was a thing in America
called the supergrid.

00:07:56.910 --> 00:07:58.200
It didn't exist.

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:00.060
But anytime you talked to anybody,

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:01.950
they would show you this
beautiful little map.

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:03.390
They'd be like, "Here's the map.

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:06.240
Here's how we're going to
build these giant long-distance

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:10.107
high-voltage DC cables
and link the country."

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:13.740
The problem is, nobody
would let anybody build

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:15.300
a long-distance power line.

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:18.100
Like, you couldn't build a
transmission line in America.

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:21.870
There is a surge of renewables being built

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:24.990
around the country and a lot
of the best renewable solar

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:28.140
and wind resources are in
places like in the central part

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:29.490
of the country.

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:31.980
But building transmission
is really difficult

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:33.003
in this country.

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:36.960
There is a patchwork of federal, state,

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:40.380
local landowner rights that
all have to be heard of.

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:42.483
Communities all have to have a say.

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:46.500
So TransWest is a
planned transmission line

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:50.130
that would take about 3,000
megawatts of wind power

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:55.130
from Wyoming into Vegas,
more than 700 miles.

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:58.863
And TransWest has been in
development for 17 years.

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:02.130
Part of the reason it's taken that long,

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:03.900
the project has had to secure permission

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:07.080
from more than 400
landowners along the route

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:11.043
and even a single holdout can
delay a project for years.

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:16.260
Ranch owner Andy Maneotis
did ultimately sell a chunk

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:17.910
of his land to TransWest,

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:20.193
but he's not happy about it.

00:09:21.030 --> 00:09:24.720
TransWest, they sent a young guy in,

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:28.080
pretty pushy, and he'd get
you flared up real quick

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:30.630
with his pushing ya, they were condemn you

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:32.220
if you didn't agree with what they wanted.

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:33.480
They're just going to take the land

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:36.060
and there wasn't nothing you
were going to do about it.

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:37.297
I have no leverage.

00:09:37.297 --> 00:09:40.200
I think they like to pick
on the guys they know

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:42.090
that not got the money to hire the lawyers

00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:43.473
to go to federal court.

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:47.070
If he waited and they actually filed

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:49.020
eminent domain papers,

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:51.720
then the value of like what he could get

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:54.810
out of that deal would fall tremendously.

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:57.900
I'm really not against
the power lines itself

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:01.260
because I understand that
people need electricity.

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:02.700
But it's the way they come in here

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:04.410
and now I'm going to be dealing with it,

00:10:04.410 --> 00:10:08.370
with construction, with
people tearing the land up.

00:10:08.370 --> 00:10:11.673
I've already got a full plate
to handle with this ranch.

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:14.580
Just this year, TransWest settled

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:16.680
with the last holdout property owners,

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:19.200
meaning that finally, after 17 years,

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:22.680
construction of the line can get started.

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:26.013
And this is one of the more
successful examples in the US.

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:28.890
There's several transmission lines

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:31.140
that have been going through the process.

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:32.640
It's been taking years.

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:35.430
They're all held up for different reasons.

00:10:35.430 --> 00:10:39.720
If we have this mandate to
clean up the grid by 2035,

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:41.610
we're going to have to figure out

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:46.386
how to speed up the
construction of transmission now

00:10:46.386 --> 00:10:48.486
to be able to build it in time.

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:56.910
Meanwhile, Asia is trying
to be less like America

00:10:56.910 --> 00:10:58.383
and more like Europe.

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:03.450
Mika Ohbayashi runs Japan's
Renewable Energy Institute,

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:05.580
which has come up with a
plan to link up the grids

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:07.803
of some of the region's biggest economies.

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:11.820
So the Asia Super Grid is a concept

00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:15.570
to integrate Asian countries.

00:11:15.570 --> 00:11:19.050
We started to focus on the
Eastern Asian countries,

00:11:19.050 --> 00:11:21.360
like China and Korea, we focus,

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:24.240
because that that in Far East Asia,

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:25.860
there is Mongolia,

00:11:25.860 --> 00:11:30.360
which has the best renewable
energy potential in the area.

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:34.590
That will benefit all Asian countries.

00:11:34.590 --> 00:11:36.840
In theory, wind and solar energy

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:39.240
from Mongolia's Gobi Desert could produce

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:42.540
as much as 2.6 terawatts of electricity,

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:47.070
more than twice the capacity
of the entire United States.

00:11:47.070 --> 00:11:48.990
The Asia Super Grid would link that power

00:11:48.990 --> 00:11:51.420
to countries that produced more than 1/3

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:53.640
of the world's CO2 emissions.

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:57.630
It's an ambitious and
potentially transformative plan,

00:11:57.630 --> 00:11:59.400
but it's not even close

00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:02.220
to the biggest idea coming out of Asia.

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:04.410
There is an idea that's
being floated by China

00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:07.620
to try and build a global grid.

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:10.260
China has a state-owned
company called State Grid

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:14.190
that has the technology
to build HVDC lines

00:12:14.190 --> 00:12:17.460
and has built many of them across China.

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:19.920
And they want to try and promote this idea

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:23.133
of a globally interconnected
electric grid.

00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:27.750
It's hard to overstate the
potential climate impact

00:12:27.750 --> 00:12:31.260
of a global grid connecting
the most renewable-rich parts

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:35.163
of the world to the most
energy-hungry population centers.

00:12:36.930 --> 00:12:41.280
But these proposals from Asia
are just that, proposals,

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:43.743
and that might not be
changing anytime soon.

00:12:45.750 --> 00:12:49.650
I have to say, politics is
the most difficult thing.

00:12:49.650 --> 00:12:52.950
We are still trying to
struggle each other,

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:54.660
competing each other.

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:56.460
Japanese government has tension

00:12:56.460 --> 00:13:00.997
with other countries that
especially China, Korea.

00:13:00.997 --> 00:13:04.830
Countries that aren't 100%
friendly aren't likely

00:13:04.830 --> 00:13:08.250
to want to link their
energy systems together.

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:11.370
And with the war in Ukraine
making European countries regret

00:13:11.370 --> 00:13:13.980
their dependency on Russian oil,

00:13:13.980 --> 00:13:17.130
any notion of a global supergrid is likely

00:13:17.130 --> 00:13:19.353
to be off the table for a good while.

00:13:20.220 --> 00:13:21.780
But the way Mika sees it,

00:13:21.780 --> 00:13:23.610
we are past the point where the actions

00:13:23.610 --> 00:13:25.800
of individual countries
can move the needle

00:13:25.800 --> 00:13:27.630
on climate change.

00:13:27.630 --> 00:13:30.600
Developed countries, like
Japan, Western World,

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:33.300
has to be renewable energy,

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:38.300
like kind of more than
60% or 70%, 80% by 2030.

00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:42.360
So, we don't have enough time to do it,

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:43.980
so I'm already saying,

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:48.000
every single cent or every single yen

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:51.120
that we invest something
has to be invested

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:54.133
for climate change actions from now on.

00:13:57.120 --> 00:13:58.530
As the grids evolved,

00:13:58.530 --> 00:14:01.953
we have had to cooperate on
larger and larger scales.

00:14:03.870 --> 00:14:07.320
Edison's DC grids served
just a few factories

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:09.060
and rich people's homes.

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:12.030
The grids of the 20th
century spanned nations,

00:14:12.030 --> 00:14:14.490
and now the grids of the 21st century

00:14:14.490 --> 00:14:16.500
would forge links between people hundreds

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:19.440
or even thousands of miles apart.

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:21.720
But if we can all get used to the idea

00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:24.000
of being a little less isolated,

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:26.700
we might find the puzzle
of decarbonization

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:29.250
gets a lot easier to solve,

00:14:29.250 --> 00:14:30.933
which would be super.

00:14:31.830 --> 00:14:34.290
If we are going to hit net zero goals

00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:39.290
and have 80, 90, or 100%
renewable power grids,

00:14:39.330 --> 00:14:42.240
then there is little chance to do that

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:44.670
without having really good transmission

00:14:44.670 --> 00:14:46.620
across long distances.

00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:49.290
If we look to our targets
and ambitions we have,

00:14:49.290 --> 00:14:51.960
we need to accelerate these processes.

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:53.640
And I think this is doable.

00:14:53.640 --> 00:14:55.830
If we enable the grid to deal

00:14:55.830 --> 00:14:58.920
with this new green energy generation,

00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:02.520
I do believe that a climate-neutral
world will be possible.

